EXT. HOLLOW BANYAN TREE - OBSERVATION POST - DAWN

A giant tree, its heart rotted out, with a crude firing slit chopped through the bark. Inside, a cramped hollow barely wide enough for two men and a field radio. Mist drifts in the jungle outside.

PRIVATE ABEBE (20s, spectacles taped at the hinge) kneels at the slit with a pair of binoculars. SERGEANT HOLLIS (40s, grey stubble, sleeves rolled) sits against the wall, shaving with a tin mirror and a cold razor.

ABEBE
Nothing.

HOLLIS
Nothing is good.

ABEBE
Nothing's been good for six hours, Sarge. I keep waiting for it to turn bad.

HOLLIS
It will. Hand me that rag.

Abebe passes a rag. Hollis wipes his chin and examines the result in the mirror.

HOLLIS (CONT'D)
You want some coffee?

ABEBE
There's coffee?

HOLLIS
There's instant. And a canteen of water that tastes like a boot.

He pours powder into a tin cup, adds water, and hands it to Abebe, who sips, winces, sips again.

ABEBE
It's terrible.

HOLLIS
Wait until you've been here a year. It'll taste like home.

ABEBE
You've been here four years.

HOLLIS
That's a long time to drink bad coffee.

Abebe laughs. It's the first laugh in the tree in days. He sits next to Hollis, lowers the binoculars. The mist thins. Far off, a bird starts up.

ABEBE
Sarge, do you think they're out there?

HOLLIS
I think they were. Yesterday.

ABEBE
What changed?

Hollis looks at him. Then, slowly, reaches for the field radio, turns the dial. Static. Then a thin, clear VOICE in English, reading a message in a loop.

Abebe goes still. Hollis lets it play.

ABEBE (CONT'D)
That's the ceasefire. That's the actual...

HOLLIS
Came through at midnight.

ABEBE
You've known for six hours?

Hollis switches off the radio. The silence is loud.

HOLLIS
I wanted him to finish his coffee before I told you.

Abebe stares at the cup in his hand, then at Hollis. He puts the cup down very carefully, as if it might break.

ABEBE
Sarge.

HOLLIS
Go on, kid. Say it.

ABEBE
(quiet)
Thank you.

Hollis looks away, rubbing his freshly shaved jaw. Outside the slit, the sun clears the trees.
